47
00:02:53,702 --> 00:02:56,994
But inside the data centers themselves, we've got the servers, of course.

48
00:02:57,019 --> 00:03:01,080
And one of the innovations that we've been developing in our servers is something called Azure Boost.

49
00:03:01,080 --> 00:03:12,360
Azure Boost is aimed at offloading a lot of the processing of the data paths for storage and networking off the main server CPUs.

50
00:03:12,360 --> 00:03:14,790
And it does this with an offload card.

51
00:03:14,790 --> 00:03:18,450
We move the data planes off onto that offload card.

52
00:03:18,450 --> 00:03:20,550
We power them with DPUs.

53
00:03:20,550 --> 00:03:24,180
We've got cores on there as well, where we put our agent tree.

54
00:03:24,180 --> 00:03:32,850
So we get the agents off the server as well, and this lets the workload have most of the control over what's going on on the server.

55
00:03:32,850 --> 00:03:34,500
This Azure Boost card looks like this.

56
00:03:34,500 --> 00:03:38,440
This is the latest generation of Azure Boost card that we've started to deploy in our data centers.

57
00:03:38,465 --> 00:03:41,580
Here's an actual version of it right here.

58
00:03:41,580 --> 00:03:46,380
And what you can see there, there's little black squares around the middle are D-RAM.

59
00:03:46,380 --> 00:03:48,420
And then the middle is where the SOC is.

60
00:03:48,420 --> 00:03:53,010
That's where the ARM cores and the DPU and FPGA are located.

61
00:03:53,010 --> 00:03:57,210
That's where the data plane gets processed and the agents run.

62
00:03:57,210 --> 00:04:00,510
Now some of the stats for Azure Boost are pretty staggering.

63
00:04:00,510 --> 00:04:03,810
You can see here, just in last year,

64
00:04:03,810 --> 00:04:11,700
since the previous generation of Azure Boost that I showed at last Build, we've gone to 20 gigabytes per second of remote storage.

65
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:15,540
And that's an increase, you can see there, of 1.4.

66
00:04:15,540 --> 00:04:21,060
We've gone to 1 million IOPS for remote storage, and on local storage, 36 gigabytes per second,

67
00:04:21,060 --> 00:04:29,224
this is to local NVMe, and 6.6 million IOPS through Azure Boost NVMe offload storage acceleration.

68
00:04:29,248 --> 00:04:33,260
And then for networking, we've gone to 400 gigabits from 200 gigabits.

69
00:04:33,260 --> 00:04:41,910
So those were two network ports that you saw there on the card, 200 gigabits each, and 400k connections per second, it supports.

70
00:04:41,910 --> 00:04:50,370
Those two ports mean that they can go to two top-of-rack router switches, so that means that we can service top-of-rack routers, or if one fails,

71
00:04:50,370 --> 00:04:52,375
we don't lose complete connectivity to the server.

72
00:04:52,400 --> 00:05:01,470
And it also means that sub-second servicing is possible now on those tours with sub-second impact to the workloads running on those servers.

73
00:05:01,470 --> 00:05:07,260
We've been deploying this for several years now, and we're over 30% of the fleet is now Azure Boost enabled.

74
00:05:07,260 --> 00:05:09,362
Every single server gets an Azure Boost in it.

75
00:05:09,387 --> 00:05:16,020
And then one of the cool innovations that I'll show you in a little bit is that we support guest virtual machine RDMA.

76
00:05:16,020 --> 00:05:25,616
So this is basically memory to memory transfers, bypassing the networking stacks, because Azure Boost is facilitating that transport.

77
00:05:25,640 --> 00:05:33,420
One of the things that I mentioned is we get the management services off the server, and we put them on the Azure Boost card.

78
00:05:33,420 --> 00:05:36,900
And that lets us have Bare Metal instances.

79
00:05:36,900 --> 00:05:46,110
We've introduced Bare Metal instances to unlimited access, specifically for OpenAI and OpenAI's use of that Fairwater data center.

80
00:05:46,110 --> 00:05:50,790
So the servers in the data center, the GPU servers, are Bare Metal instances.

81
00:05:50,790 --> 00:05:58,710
And I thought I'd show you just a quick demo of Bare Metal instances, which are going to be coming as a general purpose offering sometime in the near future.

82
00:05:58,710 --> 00:06:04,440
But here, you can see we've got a metal instance, ND144 v6.

83
00:06:04,440 --> 00:06:06,810
You can see here we're going to get the IP address,

84
00:06:06,810 --> 00:06:19,050
and then we're going to SSH into it and actually see that it is a Bare Metal server, because we're going to look to see if virtualization services are running, and they're not.

85
00:06:19,050 --> 00:06:23,670
And then we're going to look at the PCI bus using LSPCI.

86
00:06:23,670 --> 00:06:26,081
And you can see we get lots of devices.

87
00:06:26,106 --> 00:06:29,790
We're going to scroll to the top, and you can see there's a PCI bridge,

88
00:06:29,790 --> 00:06:39,150
and then you can see the eight Ethernet controllers that are connected to the Mellanox RDMA-capable network adapters

89
00:06:39,150 --> 00:06:45,360
that are attached to that for the backend network that those GPUs talk through to each other.

90
00:06:45,360 --> 00:06:46,770
Now what do they talk?

91
00:06:46,770 --> 00:06:48,300
What transport do they talk?

92
00:06:48,300 --> 00:06:53,280
We switched to Ethernet from InfiniBand, actually, in those Fairwater data centers.

93
00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:58,170
And instead of using RoCE, which is RDMA over converged Ethernet,

94
00:06:58,170 --> 00:07:03,840
we've developed, with OpenAI and now a growing consortium of partners that are collaborating with us,

95
00:07:03,840 --> 00:07:09,060
a new networking protocol called Multipath Reliable Connection, or MRC.

96
00:07:09,060 --> 00:07:16,290
And the reason that we developed MRC is that when you talk about connecting 100,000 GPUs together,

97
00:07:16,290 --> 00:07:23,220
that's an incredible amount of network fabric that those packets are going across from all of those GPUs.

98
00:07:23,220 --> 00:07:31,305
If you have any congestion or any failure on a synchronous training job of that size, you're going to lose a ton of work.

99
00:07:31,330 --> 00:07:36,660
You're going to have to go back to a checkpoint, restart the training job from where it left off.

100
00:07:36,660 --> 00:07:47,700
So MRC is designed to be resilient in the face of these congestion issues or failures because it sprays the packets across independent network domains called planes.

101
00:07:47,700 --> 00:07:56,186
And therefore, when a link gets dropped, the spraying just happens and rebalances to the planes that remain available.

102
00:07:56,211 --> 00:08:02,560
And I'll show you a quick demo of that in action by showing you here.

103
00:08:04,530 --> 00:08:11,940
What we're going to see is that I've got two servers talking to each other over four switches.

104
00:08:11,940 --> 00:08:17,130
They're sending 4 gigabyte payloads to each other.

105
00:08:17,130 --> 00:08:21,570
These 4 gigabytes are going to be packet sprayed across these.

106
00:08:21,570 --> 00:08:30,270
And you can see we're getting roughly 95 gigabytes per second, and that's because we're spraying these across eight planes with 12 gigabits.

107
00:08:30,270 --> 00:08:33,660
So that comes out to 12 gigabytes per plane.

108
00:08:33,660 --> 00:08:39,076
And this is the aggregate bandwidth that we're seeing, which is 95 gigabytes per second.

109
00:08:39,101 --> 00:08:47,160
This is what we're going to watch, because we want to maintain 95 gigabits per second there, which is our goal.

110
00:08:47,160 --> 00:08:48,510
We see four switches.

111
00:08:48,534 --> 00:08:52,391
You can see how much bandwidth is being spread across the switches.

112
00:08:52,416 --> 00:09:00,570
There's actually two switches at this bandwidth here that you don't see, so this is 12, two 24s, and one 36, adding up to 95.

113
00:09:00,570 --> 00:09:03,150
And now we're going to go kill one of those switches.

114
00:09:03,150 --> 00:09:07,260
We're going to drop it off of the sending server.

115
00:09:07,260 --> 00:09:13,290
So what it's going to do is now rebalance the spraying across the remaining three switches.

116
00:09:13,290 --> 00:09:16,530
And our goal is to not see any drop in network bandwidth.

117
00:09:16,530 --> 00:09:18,780
And, of course, that's exactly what we get.

118
00:09:18,780 --> 00:09:22,156
You can see 95-ish gigabits per second.

119
00:09:22,180 --> 00:09:27,330
And if we go take a look at the traffic over the switches, you can see that one of them went to zero,

120
00:09:27,330 --> 00:09:31,890
and then the other one went up to 36 gigabits per second to compensate for that.

121
00:09:31,890 --> 00:09:42,678
So MRC is what allows us to run these massive training jobs and has now become the industry standard for large network topologies.

122
00:09:44,680 --> 00:09:48,540
Now I mentioned RDMA a few times.

123
00:09:48,540 --> 00:09:56,160
And what I was showing you was MRC, which is what Open AI uses for their training jobs.

124
00:09:56,160 --> 00:10:00,570
And they're doing RDMA, of course, between the servers in those training jobs.

125
00:10:00,570 --> 00:10:06,950
But what we want to introduce is to more general-purpose workloads RDMA through virtual machines.

126
00:10:06,950 --> 00:10:16,806
Now when you talk between one VM and another VM through Azure Boost, they could be on different servers, you're going through these networking stacks.

127
00:10:16,830 --> 00:10:23,460
The application talks to TCP/IP, which talks to our MANA driver, the Microsoft Accelerated Network Adapter driver,

128
00:10:23,460 --> 00:10:27,900
which is the Azure Boost network adapter driver, and then it goes back up.

129
00:10:27,900 --> 00:10:33,120
And there's a lot of memory copy operations and a lot of synchronization happening through that.

130
00:10:33,120 --> 00:10:35,690
With RDMA, you bypass all that.

131
00:10:35,690 --> 00:10:40,220
So the application maps memories basically in the remote system,

132
00:10:40,220 --> 00:10:45,560
can drop data right into its own local presentation of that,

133
00:10:45,560 --> 00:10:50,460
and Azure Boost takes care of sending that and dropping it in the memory on the other side.

134
00:10:50,460 --> 00:11:00,540
And this completely eliminates a lot of that synchronization as well as the memory copies and all that software path that you get through those networking stack.

135
00:11:00,540 --> 00:11:07,020
So I'm going to show you a demo of virtual machine to virtual machine RDMA over Azure Boost.

136
00:11:07,020 --> 00:11:12,363
The scenario here is common in AI workloads, where you're doing inference.

137
00:11:12,388 --> 00:11:17,010
And inference, you can break up into two parts: prefill and decode.

138
00:11:17,010 --> 00:11:25,230
Prefill is taking your prompt and then getting the tokens from that prompt and the KV cache populated from that prompt.

139
00:11:25,230 --> 00:11:30,750
And then decode starts to generate new tokens based off of that system of that prompt.

140
00:11:30,750 --> 00:11:37,320
So it's efficient and now common to split those into different server pools, so one pool for prefill,

141
00:11:37,320 --> 00:11:43,980
one pool for decode, because they both have different constraints when it comes to memory and compute.

142
00:11:43,980 --> 00:11:53,105
So here, we're going to start this demo of... RDMA.

143
00:11:53,130 --> 00:11:56,790
So first, we're going to look at the performance over the TCP/IP stack.

144
00:11:56,790 --> 00:12:01,050
So here's the pre-filler server using TCP/IP.

145
00:12:01,050 --> 00:12:07,050
Here's the decoder server, also using TCP/IP, the way we're launching it.

146
00:12:07,050 --> 00:12:15,910
And then we're going to go run this vLLM benchmark that does this prefill decode load between those two servers.

147
00:12:17,250 --> 00:12:20,952
And then we're going to go take a look at our RDMA stats, which is in the bottom right.

148
00:12:20,977 --> 00:12:25,440
And you're going to see that they don't change as this workload runs, there's 460 send requests.

149
00:12:25,440 --> 00:12:27,900
Just keep your eye on that one.

150
00:12:27,900 --> 00:12:34,770
But then if you take a look at the performance, the token throughput, which is what we really want, is about 3,400.

151
00:12:34,770 --> 00:12:41,580
The median time to first token, which is another important stat for inference, is 123,000.

152
00:12:41,580 --> 00:12:51,570
But when we switch over to RDMA and use the MANA link for that, you can see there's the F1 Verbs Device Name as MANA 0.

153
00:12:51,570 --> 00:12:57,360
We do the same thing on the decode server, MANA 0.

154
00:12:57,360 --> 00:13:04,260
And then let's go take a look at the stats, and you can see 460, but this is going to change now, because we're running the RDMA test.

155
00:13:04,260 --> 00:13:08,670
And it changed to 506 because we've sent a whole lot of packets now over to RDMA.

156
00:13:08,670 --> 00:13:12,810
And then we've almost doubled the throughput to 7637.

157
00:13:12,810 --> 00:13:19,470
And then you can see our mean time to first token has dropped to just 47,000 milliseconds.

158
00:13:19,470 --> 00:13:29,700
So that's the performance benefit of RDMA that we're deploying and using in our inference clusters behind Foundry, powered by Azure Boost.

159
00:13:29,700 --> 00:13:32,130
Okay, so let's take a look at our core now.

160
00:13:32,130 --> 00:13:36,600
So that's kind of Fairwater infrastructure and some of the networking behind it.

161
00:13:36,600 --> 00:13:38,580
Let's go take a look at the core.

162
00:13:38,580 --> 00:13:44,405
And this is one of the sections that I get very excited about, because the future is serverless.

163
00:13:44,430 --> 00:13:46,170
And I've been saying this for a long time.

164
00:13:46,170 --> 00:13:52,979
If you've come to previous Inside Azure Innovation talks, even going back 10 years, I've been saying serverless is the future.

165
00:13:53,004 --> 00:14:00,840
Well, serverless as the future is really arriving now with Azure Container Instances, which Azure Container instances,

166
00:14:00,840 --> 00:14:09,367
the idea here is that we've got -- typically, when it comes to containers, you think about containers the way they started, which is process isolated containers.

167
00:14:09,392 --> 00:14:13,470
So they're using the operating system primitives to try to keep them separate from each other.

168
00:14:13,470 --> 00:14:15,240
But there's a problem with that.

169
00:14:15,240 --> 00:14:21,420
We don't consider a process or user mode kernel as a security boundary in Azure.

170
00:14:21,420 --> 00:14:33,630
We only consider virtualization with a very tight connection between -- small connection between the server and the host to be a security boundary in Azure.

171
00:14:33,630 --> 00:14:39,180
So with Azure Container Instances, we isolate those containers with Hyper-V.

172
00:14:39,180 --> 00:14:45,180
And this is nested hypervisor here inside a virtual machine with the containers on them.

173
00:14:45,205 --> 00:14:49,954
One of the things I'm going to show you in a little bit is where we're going with this, which is direct virtualization.

174
00:14:49,980 --> 00:14:52,140
So I'll save a description of that.

175
00:14:52,140 --> 00:15:00,849
But what I want to show you is we're making a ton of progress in adopting Azure Container Instances throughout all of not just Azure, but Microsoft services.

176
00:15:00,874 --> 00:15:04,970
I'm sure that if you take a look at this, you can see some service that you've been using,

177
00:15:04,994 --> 00:15:12,420
which means you're indirectly using Azure Container Instances, whether, for example, GitHub Actions is now running on Azure Container Instances.

178
00:15:12,420 --> 00:15:16,110
You can see Python and Excel running on Azure Container Instances.

179
00:15:16,110 --> 00:15:22,800
Horizon DB, which you might have gone to sessions about here at Build, running on Azure Container Instances.

180
00:15:22,800 --> 00:15:31,410
So one of the things that we've been doing is trying to make Container Instances more efficient and also support more scenarios and make it

181
00:15:31,410 --> 00:15:34,500
so that you never need to do process isolation.

182
00:15:34,500 --> 00:15:37,920
One of those is taking out the virtualization overhead.

183
00:15:37,920 --> 00:15:48,510
And this is what we call direct virtualization, is putting the containers right on the servers as peers to the management's virtual machine.

184
00:15:48,510 --> 00:15:52,980
We call this the parent partition or Layer 1 virtualization host.

185
00:15:52,980 --> 00:16:02,310
And these are Layer 2 virtualization hosts, or child VMs, that are simply hosting individual containers, or pods.

186
00:16:02,310 --> 00:16:08,790
And this gives us that strong hostile multi-tenant isolation for container instances.

187
00:16:08,790 --> 00:16:19,140
Now one of the other Azure Container Instances of cells now runs on top of 01 virtualization or direct virtualization,

188
00:16:19,140 --> 00:16:23,760
where we're migrating our entire fleet over to direct virtualization.

189
00:16:23,760 --> 00:16:32,790
This will probably take a year or two to migrate the whole fleet, but this is something that is going to give us a lot of performance and efficiency benefits.

190
00:16:32,790 --> 00:16:43,410
Now we want to make containers have all of the same capabilities of virtual machines, all of the same abilities to attach network devices and storage devices.

191
00:16:43,410 --> 00:16:52,650
One of the benefits you get out of a virtual machine today on any hyperscaler, including Azure, is minimal downtime when the server needs to be serviced

192
00:16:52,650 --> 00:16:56,940
or the server is going unhealthy, and we recognize that ahead of time.

193
00:16:56,940 --> 00:16:59,640
And that is with live migration.

194
00:16:59,640 --> 00:17:03,330
What's been missing with containers is support for live migration.

195
00:17:03,330 --> 00:17:09,330
So I want to show you the first public demo of Container Live Migration in Azure here,

196
00:17:09,330 --> 00:17:16,720
where we can take a bunch of containers and move them from one server over to another server.

197
00:17:20,700 --> 00:17:27,750
So here I've got a container that's running on one pool of servers.

198
00:17:27,750 --> 00:17:31,830
And here's another pool of servers over here that we're going to migrate it to.

199
00:17:31,830 --> 00:17:35,136
So this is our live migration for container instances.

200
00:17:35,161 --> 00:17:41,100
You can see this is still under development, which is why we're using these command lines here.

201
00:17:41,100 --> 00:17:45,950
And we've got this running over on Dev Box 6, Server Dev 6.

202
00:17:45,950 --> 00:17:51,080
And we've got this container workload, it's simply spitting out a tick every second.

203
00:17:51,080 --> 00:17:53,150
You can see we're up to Tick 695.

204
00:17:53,150 --> 00:18:00,180
And the migration now has kicked in and that container is no longer running on that server, which is why that stopped.

205
00:18:00,180 --> 00:18:08,580
Now if we go over and take a look at the other server, it's migrated to, Dev 1, and we look at what's running in it.

206
00:18:08,580 --> 00:18:11,760
You can see that there's our Hello World there.

207
00:18:11,760 --> 00:18:19,800
And if we go take a look at what's running inside of that, you can see there is the file that it's been dropping those ticks into.

208
00:18:19,800 --> 00:18:21,990
We're already way way up to 755.

209
00:18:21,990 --> 00:18:28,110
So let's go take a look and make sure that 695 was in there, and we didn't have any kind of gap.

210
00:18:28,110 --> 00:18:31,690
So we're going to go to a CAT, scroll back up.

211
00:18:33,240 --> 00:18:37,020
And there it is, 695 right there.

212
00:18:37,020 --> 00:18:39,420
So no gap at all.

213
00:18:39,420 --> 00:18:44,820
So container live migrating from one server to another on top of Azure Container Instance.

214
00:18:44,820 --> 00:18:46,520
By the way, feel free to clap at any time.

215
00:18:50,924 --> 00:18:52,020
All right.

216
00:18:52,020 --> 00:18:54,930
Let's switch over to talking a little bit about AI.

217
00:18:54,930 --> 00:18:59,799
I know that there's AI fatigue, but I think we're doing some cool stuff with AI that I'd like to share with you.

218
00:18:59,824 --> 00:19:09,120
And one of those is talking a little bit about what powers all of Microsoft's own inference and own training, and that's a system called Manifold.

219
00:19:09,120 --> 00:19:18,510
Manifold's idea is to treat all our pool of GPUs as one pool to do priority-based deployments of workloads

220
00:19:18,510 --> 00:19:27,750
to understand the topologies of AI-based inference and training workloads to most efficiently use that GPU capacity and to also abstract the underlying hardware.

221
00:19:27,750 --> 00:19:33,150
You can see that it supports ASICS, it supports GPUs, FPGAs, and even CPUs.

222
00:19:33,150 --> 00:19:40,920
And more and more, we have models that consist of CPU portions of the model and GPU portions of the model,

223
00:19:40,920 --> 00:19:48,660
including models that actually have mini models inside of them, like they might have an embedding model within them.

224
00:19:48,660 --> 00:19:55,552
They might have a visual large language model embedded within them, if they're a multimodal model.

225
00:19:55,577 --> 00:19:59,030
And so these topologies are getting more complicated.

226
00:19:59,030 --> 00:20:08,970
Now what we want to do is bring this power of Azure container instances in this isolation to workloads

227
00:20:08,970 --> 00:20:10,980
like this on top of direct virtualization,

228
00:20:10,980 --> 00:20:21,210
where we can put CPU containers inside of its own Hyper-V isolated container and the AI model components, those containers,

229
00:20:21,235 --> 00:20:27,770
in their own separate ones too, so they don't interfere with each other, and they get direct access to hardware at the same time.

230
00:20:27,770 --> 00:20:34,680
And this is a benefit of direct virtualization is we can assign the GPU to a virtual machine.

231
00:20:34,680 --> 00:20:44,220
That virtual machine can then create a sub-virtual machine, one of these L2s, and then give the GPUs that it's got over to that container.

232
00:20:44,220 --> 00:20:47,160
And then it has direct access to that hardware.

233
00:20:47,160 --> 00:20:52,570
So let me show you this demo of Manifold.

234
00:20:55,670 --> 00:21:05,430
And what we're going to see here is if I get the nodes in this cluster, we're going to go take a look at one of those nodes and get the pods in the node.

235
00:21:05,430 --> 00:21:12,353
And there's one pod in there, which is Llama 4 server.

236
00:21:12,378 --> 00:21:14,460
And there's another one, which is a metrics server.

237
00:21:14,460 --> 00:21:17,790
One of them is a GPU-based one, one is a CPU-based one.

238
00:21:17,790 --> 00:21:27,530
When we go take a look inside the pod that's got the GPU, the Llama model in it, you can see it's got the GPUs visible to it It's got a whole bunch of RAM visible to it.

239
00:21:27,530 --> 00:21:30,760
And it's got a whole bunch of processors assigned to it.

240
00:21:30,785 --> 00:21:36,980
And you can see the Nvidia SMI command shows us that it actually has those GPUs available to it.

241
00:21:36,980 --> 00:21:44,030
When we go take a look at the CPU pod sitting right next to it on the same server, you can see that it's got almost no memory.

242
00:21:44,030 --> 00:21:45,890
It's only got two processors.

243
00:21:45,890 --> 00:21:48,786
And it's got no GPUs attached to it.

244
00:21:48,811 --> 00:21:50,190
And just to prove to you it works,

245
00:21:50,190 --> 00:22:01,680
we're going to go take a look at this cat video and send this for inference to that GPU pod sitting there with direct access to GPUs, the Llama 4 Maverick model there,

246
00:22:01,680 --> 00:22:06,150
and then successfully do an inference there.

247
00:22:06,150 --> 00:22:19,520
The point being proven here that we've got CPU pods completely isolated from GPU pods directly attached to the physical server hardware because of direct virtualization.

248
00:22:24,653 --> 00:22:26,880
Now we're also not just optimizing the compute.

249
00:22:26,880 --> 00:22:37,890
We're also optimizing the way that inference works in terms of KV caches, which is -- the KV cache is effectively the state of the LLM.

250
00:22:37,890 --> 00:22:48,030
And you can't do decoding efficiently without having a KV cache which represents the processing of all the previous tokens in the prompt.

251
00:22:48,030 --> 00:22:51,728
When you interact with an LLM, you do so typically using turns.

252
00:22:51,753 --> 00:22:54,780
And so you might have Turn 1, where you just have a system prompt.

253
00:22:54,780 --> 00:22:58,380
Let's say you're doing a PR diff because you're doing development.

254
00:22:58,380 --> 00:23:04,380
And you engage in an interaction with Copilot on that PR diff.

255
00:23:04,380 --> 00:23:11,310
And what that means is that you're extending the prompt effectively, your specific prompt.

256
00:23:11,310 --> 00:23:14,010
In an ideal case, you don't just want to cache the system prompt.

257
00:23:14,010 --> 00:23:23,790
And this is what a lot of AI frameworks let you do is they say, hey, cache this for me, and then I'm not going to have to pay the cost for decoding this again or prefilling it.

258
00:23:23,790 --> 00:23:26,760
But what you really want is the whole thing to be cached,

259
00:23:26,760 --> 00:23:35,100
so that if your workload moves, your inference moves from one GP to another, one server to another, that you're not repaying this price.

260
00:23:35,100 --> 00:23:42,810
And we can only do this if we've got a cache that sits all the way behind all of the servers.

261
00:23:42,810 --> 00:23:46,710
So this is something that we've got coming out in preview now called Azure Context Cache.

262
00:23:46,710 --> 00:23:52,260
The idea here is that Azure Context Cache, behind it, has a storage account.

263
00:23:52,260 --> 00:23:58,860
And that storage account is where we're caching the KV state for all of the inference requests.

264
00:23:58,860 --> 00:24:01,500
So regardless of what server they go to,

265
00:24:01,500 --> 00:24:13,410
if the prompt is not there, the KV isn't there, we can look it up in the cache and fetch it in and not have to decode that again, saving time and money.

266
00:24:13,410 --> 00:24:17,140
So let's go take a look at that Azure Context Cache in action.

267
00:24:19,620 --> 00:24:28,650
So here, we're sending, concurrency level one, we're sending a bunch of queries into the system, all with the same system prompt.

268
00:24:28,650 --> 00:24:31,140
And you can see that we had all of those cached.

269
00:24:31,140 --> 00:24:39,736
The cache hit rate was 81%, because after the first request goes to the server, the next one is likely gonna have that system prompt already cached in it.

270
00:24:39,761 --> 00:24:43,773
But when we do a concurrency level of 10, they're going to get sprayed across servers,

271
00:24:43,797 --> 00:24:50,070
and our cache hit rate actually goes down because now sometimes a request is landing on a server without the system prompt.

272
00:24:50,070 --> 00:24:57,720
So a lot of systems have you set this cache key unique to try to pin a conversation on a particular server, an endpoint.

273
00:24:57,720 --> 00:25:06,420
The hit rate actually drops there because every conversation gets mapped to a different server intentionally where the system prompt might not have already shown up.

274
00:25:06,420 --> 00:25:10,980
So the first hit there is always -- the first query there is always going to be a miss.

275
00:25:10,980 --> 00:25:21,000
But when we set the prompt cache, you can see that the prompt cache rate, hit rate is going to go up.

276
00:25:21,000 --> 00:25:23,400
Where did the demo go here?

277
00:25:23,400 --> 00:25:28,710
Went up to 96% with the remote cache here; 96.2% is the hit rate here.

278
00:25:28,710 --> 00:25:34,140
And that's because no matter which server the query lands on,

279
00:25:34,140 --> 00:25:40,740
the prompt is -- the KV cache for that prompt is going to be fetched from remote cache if it wasn't there.

280
00:25:40,740 --> 00:25:48,300
So transparently, behind the scenes, this prompt cache using Azure storage to go and spread out the KV cache across the servers,

281
00:25:48,300 --> 00:25:57,210
effectively making the servers have a pool cache rather than individual server-based caches, which is going to improve hit rate, which is going to save money,

282
00:25:57,210 --> 00:26:03,780
so you're not having to pay for the prefill on those system caches every time you have a miss.

283
00:26:03,780 --> 00:26:04,950
All right.

284
00:26:04,950 --> 00:26:08,790
So let's take a look at the next cool innovation.

285
00:26:08,790 --> 00:26:15,210
And I've got a -- it's like an ACI-themed talk, because this is one of the cool things about ACI.

286
00:26:15,210 --> 00:26:19,399
When you talk about containers, you're talking about layers and images.

287
00:26:19,424 --> 00:26:24,750
When you talk about virtual machines, you're talking about full-blown virtual machine images.

288
00:26:24,750 --> 00:26:34,380
Now, when you talk about container sandboxes, you're typically talking about something that is very bespoke, kind of a custom image, and it's very small and lightweight.

289
00:26:34,380 --> 00:26:37,140
And this is what you want to be extremely fast.

290
00:26:37,140 --> 00:26:46,350
Now one of the ways to get a fast startup for a container image, which you want with these for these agents to be able to say, "I'm going to go write a Python script

291
00:26:46,350 --> 00:26:55,590
and run it, I'm going to go create a little HTML site and iterate on it," is you want the startup to be close to instantaneous.

292
00:26:55,590 --> 00:27:02,370
When it comes to containers, you can get startup times in the five, 20-second range, depending on what the OS is.

293
00:27:02,370 --> 00:27:11,550
But we want literally thousands of these things to be able to start simultaneously and to start very quickly off of the same base image.

294
00:27:11,550 --> 00:27:14,040
The way we do that is with memory snapshotting.

295
00:27:14,040 --> 00:27:20,340
So we actually start the container, snapshot its memory, and then if you get a request for the same container,

296
00:27:20,340 --> 00:27:26,970
we simply map the memory, and we skip all the initialization and map it right into a running container box.

297
00:27:26,970 --> 00:27:30,090
So let me show you one of, I think, my favorite demo of this,

298
00:27:30,090 --> 00:27:37,886
which is, how would you like to see 10,000 container sandboxes launch?

299
00:27:39,540 --> 00:27:44,580
Okay, because if the answer is yes, that's the rest of the session.

300
00:27:44,580 --> 00:27:46,626
I'm just kidding, no, I'm just kidding.

301
00:27:46,651 --> 00:27:51,630
What I'm going to show you is that we can do this incredibly quickly.

302
00:27:51,630 --> 00:27:56,970
So I've got 10 workers launching 1,000 of these container sandboxes each.

303
00:27:56,970 --> 00:28:02,730
And we've got a little animation to show you the progress of those launches here.

304
00:28:02,730 --> 00:28:03,930
So here we go.

305
00:28:03,930 --> 00:28:06,310
Create 10 worker pods.

306
00:28:10,650 --> 00:28:12,960
Here we go.

307
00:28:12,960 --> 00:28:15,270
So each one is going to add 1,000.

308
00:28:15,270 --> 00:28:17,460
This represents 1,000.

309
00:28:17,460 --> 00:28:18,876
You can see them launching.

310
00:28:20,705 --> 00:28:23,376
A few down here left.

311
00:28:23,400 --> 00:28:25,170
Boom, done.

312
00:28:25,170 --> 00:28:29,460
10,000 container sandboxes.

313
00:28:31,840 --> 00:28:37,140
Average container sandbox launch time, two seconds.

314
00:28:37,140 --> 00:28:40,410
So not just speed, but scale at the same time.

315
00:28:40,410 --> 00:28:43,380
So this is, like I said, one of the really cool demos.

316
00:28:43,380 --> 00:28:50,926
And so you're going to see the agent sandboxing land on top of Azure Container Instance.

317
00:28:50,951 --> 00:28:52,108
All right.

318
00:28:52,133 --> 00:28:58,410
Let's go into our last section now and talk about, or second to last section, and talk a little bit about security.

319
00:28:58,410 --> 00:29:06,823
And one of the exciting things for me about security is the evolution of something called confidential computing.

320
00:29:06,848 --> 00:29:09,180
How many people have heard of confidential computing?

321
00:29:09,180 --> 00:29:10,530
So, quite a few of you.

322
00:29:10,530 --> 00:29:18,570
For those of you that haven't heard of confidential computing, the easiest way to think about it is in the context of how your data gets protected.

323
00:29:18,570 --> 00:29:26,220
And for a long time now, everybody encrypts their network traffic.

324
00:29:26,220 --> 00:29:32,070
Over the last 20 years or so, it went from every now and then you'd run into a site with SSL.

325
00:29:32,070 --> 00:29:37,800
And now every single site, every single network connection has to have TLS on it.

326
00:29:37,800 --> 00:29:42,210
Then, the other part of protecting is while it's at rest.

327
00:29:42,210 --> 00:29:44,580
And this is where it's stored on disk.

328
00:29:44,580 --> 00:29:46,080
You want to have that encrypted, too,

329
00:29:46,080 --> 00:29:55,170
so if the storage gets accessed outside of the protection of the logical protections of the workload on top of it, that the data is useless.

330
00:29:55,170 --> 00:29:57,570
And we not only have now in cloud.

331
00:29:57,570 --> 00:29:59,669
We started with node data encryption at rest.

332
00:29:59,694 --> 00:30:01,470
And now we've got server-side encryption.

333
00:30:01,470 --> 00:30:05,975
We also have customer managed key, where you can bring your own encryption and have double layered encryption of data at rest.

334
00:30:06,000 --> 00:30:16,620
But what's been missing largely is protecting the data while it's in use, because when that data gets decrypted on the server or pulled off the disk in clear text, it's sitting

335
00:30:16,620 --> 00:30:22,860
in there on the server, maybe inside of a virtual machine, but still in the server environment.

336
00:30:22,860 --> 00:30:34,140
And what can access that data includes everything from the hypervisor to the admins to the host software to a malicious hacker that might penetrate the infrastructure.

337
00:30:34,140 --> 00:30:36,000
All of those can get access.

338
00:30:36,000 --> 00:30:38,310
And even in your own applications,

339
00:30:38,310 --> 00:30:48,030
you want to keep maybe your own operators away from that data and only have the compute that needs to have access to that data have access to that data.

340
00:30:48,030 --> 00:30:49,380
That is confidential computing.

341
00:30:49,380 --> 00:30:57,000
Now one aspect of confidential computing that is not just this protection of that data in a hardware enclave,

342
00:30:57,000 --> 00:31:04,380
where none of those things can get inside of it, is that the hardware also can measure what's inside the enclave.

343
00:31:04,380 --> 00:31:06,720
And that measurement is called an attestation.

344
00:31:06,720 --> 00:31:14,790
And some code inside that enclave can ask the hardware for a quote, which is the attestation, present it to a relying party,

345
00:31:14,790 --> 00:31:24,420
which is typically a key management service that has a policy on it that says only release this key to an enclave of this hardware type

346
00:31:24,420 --> 00:31:29,250
with the security version number on it with this configuration of code.

347
00:31:29,250 --> 00:31:31,380
And at that point, the key gets released.

348
00:31:31,380 --> 00:31:34,590
It's encrypted to the enclave, the code in the enclave.

349
00:31:34,590 --> 00:31:43,890
The code in the enclave decrypts the key and then can start to process the data in the clear, and everybody knows that it's being protected with that hardware isolation.

350
00:31:43,890 --> 00:31:45,630
That is confidential computing.

351
00:31:45,630 --> 00:31:54,240
We've been working on enabling confidential computing in Azure for over a decade, starting with Intel SGX servers

352
00:31:54,240 --> 00:32:00,570
in the data center, and now virtual machines from AMD and Intel in the data center.

353
00:32:00,570 --> 00:32:05,550
And our portfolio is the largest of any hyperscaler when it comes to confidential computing services,

354
00:32:05,550 --> 00:32:13,753
confidential computing container offerings like confidential containers on ACI, as well as the hardware infrastructure underneath it.

355
00:32:16,080 --> 00:32:18,270
I talked before about live migration.

356
00:32:18,270 --> 00:32:28,530
And live migration is one of those things that allows us to run the fleet efficiently, because we can move workloads around to reduce fragmentation.

357
00:32:28,530 --> 00:32:34,200
We can also make customer workloads more reliable because we can move them if we see a server is going to fail.

358
00:32:34,200 --> 00:32:38,760
We can also move them around if we need to service a host in an impactful way.

359
00:32:38,760 --> 00:32:44,610
And that means that the customer's workload is not as impacted by that servicing operation.

360
00:32:44,610 --> 00:32:50,010
It is a fundamental requirement of a hyperscale cloud to have live migration.

361
00:32:50,010 --> 00:32:57,390
And this has unfortunately been one of the missing gaps in confidential computing offerings is lack of live migration.

362
00:32:57,390 --> 00:33:03,930
So we've been working deeply with Intel on confidential live migration.

363
00:33:03,930 --> 00:33:08,040
And the way that works is you have a confidential virtual machine here on one server.

364
00:33:08,040 --> 00:33:14,850
We've got something called a migration TD, which is a partition, effectively.

365
00:33:14,850 --> 00:33:24,570
Think of it as a confidential partition that is also measured as part of the virtual machine's measurement and that the receiving side has a policy and the sending side

366
00:33:24,570 --> 00:33:35,430
as a policy that says I will only migrate the contents of this virtual machine to another server with the MigTD that is approved for this virtual machine,

367
00:33:35,430 --> 00:33:45,060
so effectively extending the confidential trust boundary between two servers, adhering to policy that is being enforced

368
00:33:45,060 --> 00:33:46,860
with the confidential computing hardware.

369
00:33:46,860 --> 00:33:53,790
I'm going to show you for the first time ever a confidential virtual machine being live migrated from one server to another.

370
00:33:53,790 --> 00:33:58,030
This is the first time ever this has been shown publicly.

371
00:34:00,090 --> 00:34:04,615
So here, we have one server here, Hyper-V manager, you can see, a lot of you are familiar with it.

372
00:34:04,640 --> 00:34:06,030
There's our CVM.

373
00:34:06,030 --> 00:34:09,300
Here's the other server manager on the other side.

374
00:34:09,300 --> 00:34:11,220
You can see no virtual machines there.

375
00:34:11,220 --> 00:34:14,100
You can see there's our MigTD that we have in this virtual machine.

376
00:34:14,100 --> 00:34:21,810
And here we're looking at the output of RDP into that virtual machine on Server 1.

377
00:34:21,810 --> 00:34:23,790
And you can see it's pinging every second.

378
00:34:23,790 --> 00:34:25,950
That's typical in a live migration demo.

379
00:34:25,950 --> 00:34:28,070
Now we started migrating this virtual machine.

380
00:34:28,070 --> 00:34:36,630
You can see this is going to take a while, so we're going to speed it up a little bit, because this is a large virtual machine with a few gigabytes of RAM.

381
00:34:36,630 --> 00:34:41,078
You can see the TD info attestations that are happening that we've got printed out.

382
00:34:41,103 --> 00:34:44,100
And then boom, the CVM just moved,

383
00:34:44,100 --> 00:34:52,550
and it's on the other side, which is why we saw that RDP connection die, because it's connected to the IP address on that first machine.

384
00:34:52,550 --> 00:35:01,620
But that right there, first time ever, and it's not in production yet, but you can see that we just literally achieved this in the last few weeks,

385
00:35:01,620 --> 00:35:05,520
the first confidential virtual machine line migration anywhere.

386
00:35:10,458 --> 00:35:16,530
Now the next thing I'm going to show you is something that I also think is really, really cool.

387
00:35:16,530 --> 00:35:19,553
And this is something else that Microsoft has pioneered.

388
00:35:19,578 --> 00:35:24,430
We've been working on hardware-based roots of trust for a while.

389
00:35:24,455 --> 00:35:27,810
We've got something called Caliptra that we donated to the OpenCUBE project.

390
00:35:27,810 --> 00:35:31,800
It is our root of trust that we deploy in every single server.

391
00:35:31,800 --> 00:35:38,693
But when I was working with the architects, hardware security architects in Azure,

392
00:35:38,718 --> 00:35:43,320
and thinking, you know what we're missing it's not just a root of trust,

393
00:35:43,320 --> 00:35:54,210
but we need key management inside of our hardware that we put into every server, because what that will allow us to do is keep keys protected

394
00:35:54,210 --> 00:36:01,500
with hardware throughout their whole life cycle, never have them exit the hardware trust boundary.

395
00:36:01,500 --> 00:36:05,490
And so we developed something internally called MANA Corp.

396
00:36:05,490 --> 00:36:16,370
Publicly, we announced it late last year as Azure Integrated HSM, because it's effectively an HSM sitting there inside of every single server.

397
00:36:16,370 --> 00:36:21,570
And we've been deploying these now into every single server in Azure.

398
00:36:21,570 --> 00:36:29,880
What this allows is a key to be released with policy from an actual HSM.

399
00:36:29,880 --> 00:36:39,510
So like Azure-managed HSM or Azure Key Vault can have a policy on it that will release the keys into Azure Integrated HSMs.

400
00:36:39,510 --> 00:36:51,080
And Azure Integrated HSMs are FIPS 140 Level 3, FIPS 140-3, level 3, certified just like the HSMs behind Manage HSM and Azure Key Vault.

401
00:36:51,080 --> 00:37:00,270
And so that means that the key remains inside of a FIPS 140-3 Level 3 boundary from the HSM to the server.

402
00:37:00,270 --> 00:37:07,883
And then it can be accessed for operations directly from that virtual machine, because we map it, that hardware, into the virtual machine.

403
00:37:09,350 --> 00:37:23,940
Let's go see a demo of that and the benefits that you get out of that, using a simple scenario, which is I want to sign a bunch of payloads using my AKV key.

404
00:37:23,940 --> 00:37:31,380
So what I've got here is a policy that allows that key to be transported into Azure Integrated HSM.

405
00:37:31,380 --> 00:37:42,410
What I'm going to do first is sign 5,000 times over 32 threads a payload using Azure AKV, where the key never exits the HSM.

406
00:37:42,435 --> 00:37:49,530
And you can see because we're sending all these operations to the HSM, we get about 640 operations per second.

407
00:37:49,530 --> 00:37:53,910
Now we release that key into the local Azure integrated HSM.

408
00:37:53,910 --> 00:37:56,220
We can perform those operations directly on it.

409
00:37:56,220 --> 00:38:02,450
And you can see that our throughput went to 18,800 signing operations per second.

410
00:38:02,450 --> 00:38:06,510
And we lost no security in the process.

411
00:38:06,510 --> 00:38:13,230
And so this is the future of data center infrastructure and security for keys.

412
00:38:13,230 --> 00:38:16,800
The future we're moving to is one with confidential computing,

413
00:38:16,800 --> 00:38:23,450
where keys, and that's going to end up being keys for everything, keys for any of these services,

414
00:38:23,450 --> 00:38:31,550
including the services that agents are talking to, are going to be protected with hardware throughout their entire life cycle so that it can never be stolen.

415
00:38:31,550 --> 00:38:33,520
And that's a very exciting future.

416
00:38:38,905 --> 00:38:39,990
All right.

417
00:38:39,990 --> 00:38:44,250
We've got one last thing to show, and this is very experimental.

418
00:38:44,250 --> 00:38:48,540
But I like to end with, hey, this is where we're looking speculatively.

419
00:38:48,540 --> 00:38:54,840
This is Microsoft Research work that is happening, may or may not be ever productized,

420
00:38:54,840 --> 00:39:01,260
but it looks really promising, and it really would be huge if we were able to make this happen.

421
00:39:01,260 --> 00:39:11,910
And the idea here is that if you take a look at network communications, networking inside of a data center, it's typically been over copper.

422
00:39:11,910 --> 00:39:16,110
And the problem with copper, it's very lightweight, it's very cheap, it's very reliable,

423
00:39:16,110 --> 00:39:24,630
but the maximum distances you can talk to over copper without loss is about 2 meters.

424
00:39:24,630 --> 00:39:29,121
And, of course, 2 meters is not much distance in the data center, you really want to connect,

425
00:39:29,146 --> 00:39:35,850
especially if you move towards disaggregated devices and computing, where you want GPUs over here and you want servers over here,

426
00:39:35,850 --> 00:39:41,340
is that you want a very high speed, high bandwidth interconnect.

427
00:39:41,340 --> 00:39:42,990
And that's typically not copper.

428
00:39:42,990 --> 00:39:47,640
Now what a lot of people are moving towards is optics, and optics with lasers.

429
00:39:47,640 --> 00:39:55,140
The problem with lasers is that they're very high powered, so they generate a lot of heat, which makes them very expensive.

430
00:39:55,140 --> 00:40:01,860
So what we've been exploring is something called Project Mosaic.

431
00:40:01,860 --> 00:40:08,760
Now one of the things with this mechanism that I just showed you is that there's lots of fiber -- there's just a few fibers.

432
00:40:08,760 --> 00:40:11,760
They're all very high bandwidth and high speed.

433
00:40:11,760 --> 00:40:17,310
What we're trying is using many channels operating at low speed,

434
00:40:17,310 --> 00:40:31,740
using standard LED technology, micro LEDs, the kind of LEDs that are being productized right now for next generation VR glasses, for example, goggles, as well as cameras.

435
00:40:31,740 --> 00:40:41,000
These things -- projecting those LED lights over fibers inside of a cable and then having them read

436
00:40:41,000 --> 00:40:43,890
with standard silicon camera sensor arrays.

437
00:40:43,890 --> 00:40:53,356
So the beauty of this approach is that these are basically commercial technologies that are scaling because of other economic factors outside

438
00:40:53,381 --> 00:40:55,320
of what we want to take advantage of it for.

439
00:40:55,320 --> 00:40:59,820
So we can kind of ride on the back of this technology, but deploy it in our data centers.

440
00:40:59,820 --> 00:41:11,520
So I'm going to show you for the first time ever, with a live feed from our Microsoft Research Cambridge Lab, transmission over one of these LED cables.

441
00:41:11,520 --> 00:41:13,470
So let's go and switch here...

442
00:41:17,583 --> 00:41:19,380
to Microsoft Cambridge.

443
00:41:19,380 --> 00:41:22,170
And Katar, good to see you.

444
00:41:22,195 --> 00:41:23,823
Katar: Hello, Mark, good to see you.

445
00:41:23,848 --> 00:41:25,770
Hello, everyone, actually, I'm Katar.

446
00:41:25,770 --> 00:41:28,140
I'm from Microsoft Research, Cambridge.

447
00:41:28,140 --> 00:41:33,260
I'm here in the lab, holding one of those cables with micro IDs.

448
00:41:33,260 --> 00:41:42,930
And what I wanted to show you exactly is what is inside and how this is being tested in the lab before it goes into Microsoft data centers.

449
00:41:42,930 --> 00:41:52,950
So if you open this up, this is what we're going to see: a small PCB with the yellow area here, which we call the package.

450
00:41:52,950 --> 00:41:58,980
And on top of that we have a tiny array of micro-LEDs and photo detectors.

451
00:41:58,980 --> 00:42:04,620
And underneath, we have the CMOS chip that controls everything.

452
00:42:04,620 --> 00:42:12,450
And actually, this small PCB is being tested here in the lab using the evaluation board, the big PCB that we see there.

453
00:42:12,450 --> 00:42:23,610
And the green light coming up is actually coming from hundreds of micro-LEDs and coupled to this imaging fiber using this optical coupler.

454
00:42:23,610 --> 00:42:32,280
Actually, after tens of meters of transmission over this imaging fiber, here we are at the receiver side.

455
00:42:32,280 --> 00:42:37,500
And the screen here shows the output process of the fiber.

456
00:42:37,500 --> 00:42:42,210
And actually, this fiber itself has more than thousands of pores.

457
00:42:42,210 --> 00:42:50,580
And each of these micro-LEDs actually is coupled to more than one pore, which makes the alignment much more easier.

458
00:42:50,580 --> 00:42:54,180
And, actually, in a real operation mode,

459
00:42:54,180 --> 00:43:01,320
all this micro-LED will be armed and flashing at high speed, billion times per second.

460
00:43:01,320 --> 00:43:05,460
And the human eye will not be able to distinguish this.

461
00:43:05,460 --> 00:43:11,400
So basically, to make it concrete, and to show you that we can individually control each of these micro-LEDS,

462
00:43:11,400 --> 00:43:19,693
I'm going to ask Mark to pick any letter or a number from zero to nine, and I can display it to you.

463
00:43:19,718 --> 00:43:21,013
Mark Russinovich: Okay, awesome.

464
00:43:21,038 --> 00:43:23,148
This kind of proves that it's live -- how about a letter?

465
00:43:23,173 --> 00:43:24,834
Somebody shout out a letter that we want to see.

466
00:43:24,859 --> 00:43:27,360
Z? Z, all right, let's do Z.

467
00:43:28,359 --> 00:43:30,863
Katar: Z, Okay.

468
00:43:32,250 --> 00:43:37,020
Okay, so here, you can see that we are switching off some of the micro-LEDs.

469
00:43:37,020 --> 00:43:45,270
And each of these flowers here are one micro-LED that can be modulated as gigabit per second.

470
00:43:45,270 --> 00:43:49,510
So let's wait for the letter Z to show.

471
00:43:51,963 --> 00:43:53,306
Yeah.

472
00:43:55,289 --> 00:43:56,496
Mark Russinovich: Z.

473
00:43:59,698 --> 00:44:03,333
Katar: Hope you enjoyed this short demo, And I'm going to hand it back to you, Mark.

474
00:44:03,358 --> 00:44:04,770
Mark Russinovich: All right, thanks, that was awesome.

475
00:44:04,770 --> 00:44:05,850
Thanks, Katar.

476
00:44:05,850 --> 00:44:07,300
Thank you.

477
00:44:08,633 --> 00:44:13,740
